From: BMC firmware
Subject: cmm: a UDP connection goes to the forward engine after its first reply

The daemon handed the sending direction of a UDP connection to the
forward engine as soon as conntrack had the connection, before the other
side had sent anything. Every packet after the first then went through
the hardware, past netfilter.

ddos_protect limits UDP packets of connections without a reply
(ctstate NEW, 100 a second by default) and relies on such packets
reaching netfilter (ADR-0011). On the board, of 5000 packets sent
through the router with one 5-tuple at 500 a second, the rule saw one
(logs/t22-flood-2.log, 2026-10-09).

Now the sending direction waits for the first reply; the answering
direction waits for ASSURED as before. UDP that is never answered stays
on the CPU.

---
--- a/cmm/src/conntrack.c
+++ b/cmm/src/conntrack.c
@@ -2422,7 +2422,11 @@
 				if (status & IPS_ASSURED) {
 					rc = __cmmCtRegister(ctx->fci_handle, ctx->handle, ct, ctEntry, ORIGINATOR | REPLIER);
 				}
-				else if (status & IPS_CONFIRMED) {
+				else if ((status & (IPS_CONFIRMED | IPS_SEEN_REPLY)) == (IPS_CONFIRMED | IPS_SEEN_REPLY)) {
+					/* Not before the other side has answered: a
+					 * flood of UDP packets with one 5-tuple would
+					 * go through the forward engine after its first
+					 * packet, past the limit of ddos_protect. */
 					rc = __cmmCtRegister(ctx->fci_handle, ctx->handle, ct, ctEntry, ORIGINATOR);
 				}
 				else {
